  "reasoning": "The claim that MSG causes headaches, numbness, and other symptoms associated with so-called \"Chinese restaurant syndrome\" has been thoroughly debunked by scientific research. The term originated in 1968 following an anecdotal letter sent to the New England Journal of Medicine. Since then, numerous rigorous, double-blind, placebo-controlled clinical trials have consistently failed to establish a causal link between dietary MSG consumption and these symptoms. Food safety and health authorities worldwide, including the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and the World Health Organization (WHO), classify MSG as \"generally recognized as safe\" (GRAS). Chemically, the glutamate in MSG is identical to the glutamate naturally found in foods such as tomatoes, cheese, and mushrooms.",
